# Iterator

Walk a collection without exposing how it is stored. Different iterators = different traversals (price order, category, pages).

## Anatomy

- `hasNext` / `next` / `reset` (or implement `Iterable`).
- Concrete iterators hold **cursor state**.
- Collection exposes factory methods for each traversal.

```ts
export interface Iterator<T> {
  hasNext(): boolean;
  next(): T;
}

export class PriceAscendingIterator implements Iterator<Product> {
  private position = 0;
  private readonly sorted: Product[];

  constructor(products: Product[]) {
    this.sorted = [...products].sort((a, b) => a.price - b.price);
  }

  hasNext() {
    return this.position < this.sorted.length;
  }

  next() {
    const item = this.sorted[this.position];
    this.position += 1;
    return item;
  }
}
```

Iterators are **not** providers — they are per-traversal.

## Nest mapping

Prefer language tools:

```ts
for (const file of files) { /* … */ }
files.filter((f) => f.status === SpaceFileStatus.READY)
```

Drizzle already returns arrays/cursors. `list-space-files.use-case.ts` should return rows, not a custom iterator class.

Use a named iterator only when you have **several non-trivial traversals** of an in-memory aggregate and want to hide the tree (Composite + Iterator).

JS `Symbol.iterator` / async generators are the idiomatic form.

## When to use

- Multiple traversals; collection class must stay dumb.
- Hide a tree or graph.

## When not to use

- One `for` / `map` over an array.
- Pagination belongs in SQL (`limit`/`offset` or cursors), not an in-memory `PaginatedIterator` of the full table.

Related: `nest-composite.mdc`, `nest-factory-method.mdc`, `nest-visitor.mdc`.
